   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to treatment programs\nand treatment court during interim probation supervision",
    "basePrintNo": "S5450",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5450-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §390.30, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5450\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the criminal procedure law, in\nrelation to treatment programs and treatment court during interim\nprobation supervision\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo extend the two-year exception for interim probation period to a\ndefendant in any type of treatment program supervised by any treatment\ncourt.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 390.30(6) (a) of the Criminal\nProcedure Law to create a two-year interim probation period for any\ntreatment program supervised by any treatment court.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nSee CPL 390.30.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder the Criminal Procedure Law, interim probation is permitted during\nthe pre-sentence period. Criminal Procedure Law article 390 authorizes\nan interim probation period between the date of the plea and the date of\nsentencing for a one-year time period, but the statute explicitly\ncreates an exception of a two-year period only in cases where the\ndefendant is in a substance abuse treatment program that is supervised\nby a drug court.\n\nThis bill seeks to expand the interim probation exception so that any\ntreatment program supervised by any treatment court can have up to two\nyears of interim probation. For example, under current law, a mental\nhealth treatment program supervised by a Mental Health Court or a veter-\nan's treatment program supervised by a Veterans' Court can only be part\nof the interim probation for one year. Under this bill, however, the\ninterim probation period could last for two years. It would benefit a\nparticipant in a mental health treatment program to have an additional\nyear of interim probation because there would be a continuation of\nresources, which would be more stable for the participant and more effi-\ncient in terms of delivery of services. Veterans treatment tracks often\nassist veterans in getting treatment for substance abuse and PTSD, and\nprovides support services tackling unemployment, homelessness, and\nre-adjustment to civilian life. In addition, peer mentors are offered,\nand volunteer veterans help their fellow veterans to stay on track with\ntheir program.  If a participant would require additional time for\ntreatment, the participant could no longer be prevented from receiving\nsuch treatment under an interim probation program.\n\nWestchester's new hub court system provides non-violent offenders and\nmilitary veterans throughout the county the opportunity to receive the\nhelp they need and to avoid a criminal record, which would prevent them\nfrom moving forward with their lives. This program has been successful\nin the larger cities in the county, reducing incarceration and recidiv-\nism rates. The interim probation provisions set out in the Criminal\nProcedure Law could provide additional help to these courts, which as\nhub courts, have jurisdiction to help many people who have specific,\nspecialized needs.\n\nThe New York Legislature should pass the bill because it would serve as\nanother tool in the law enforcement toolbox that allows a defendant the\nopportunity to undergo a necessary treatment program for additional time\nif needed. By extending the time of interim probation to two years,\ntreatment courts will have more flexibility to meet the needs of the\ndefendants before them and to more efficiently treat their complicated\nissues.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nNone.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
